# Current rule and authoring decisions

The original rule sorts the multiset of bar heights left-to-right, retaining every bar's horizontal interval and color. It does not sort colors, areas, or whole objects. Magenta (6) is the baseline and cyan (8) is the background. Separate same-colored bars remain separate; adjacent differently colored bars are distinct. A height of 1 is legal. Even heights use top = baseline - floor(height/2), so they extend one cell farther upward. The provided test establishes this even-height convention. All three demonstrations and supplied test gold match the general rule.

The native `order` parameter is `ascending` (exact reference.rkt bytes) or `descending`. Its input-dependent dependency is: extract bar intervals/colors/heights and the baseline, then sort the measured multiset and reconstruct centered rectangles using the ordered lengths. A wrong intermediate height vector changes the final grid. No supplied-grid lookup is used.

## Grammar and generation

Rectangular 1..30 grids, one magenta baseline and solid, aligned bars with colors other than 6/8. Generated episodes have 3–8 bars of varied widths, gaps, colors, repeated heights and repeated nonadjacent colors. Both odd and even heights occur. Requests support 2–6 demos and 1–4 queries. RNG streams are independent of settings and requested counts. The entire four-query bank is shuffled before slicing. Inputs and query order are identical across settings. The 30×30 stress has eight bars and at least 250 non-cyan cells.

The first two lessons cover an offset baseline, a dense eight-bar case, varied widths, duplicate heights and colors. At seed 1, the original sampler accidentally placed both demo baselines at the canvas midpoint. A wrong fixed-midpoint rule matched both demos but failed a query. `tests/fixed_middle_counterexample.json` preserves two matching demonstrations and the failing query with six protected-Racket executions. The first demo is now constructively offset; the regression and 32-seed finite audit reject this shortcut.

## Checks and boundaries

Ten executable rivals: identity, reverse, area order, color order, deduplicated heights, unit width, first-four only, height cap 13, fixed midpoint, and top alignment. The bounded audit validates their Python and Racket implementations against each other and checks matched inputs, all 20 supported request-count combinations, and hidden-field invariance. Finite rivals are not proof of universal lesson uniqueness. The grammar excludes noisy or disconnected bars, arbitrary baselines, and vertical-axis scenes; these are not silently approximated.

`authoring_tools.py audit --seeds 32 --out ...` runs the reusable audit. `export --preflight ... --out ...` and `verify-export --preflight ... --bundle ...` bind one-query exports to the current shared preflight validator. Filenames hash only visible bytes; private order and provenance stay private. Exports refuse duplicates/existing destinations before writing. Filesystem directory iteration order is not a presentation contract; the manifest is explicitly sorted and the controller sends one selected file.

Authoring evidence is unapproved. Owner registration, exact-source native qualification/acceptance and training admission remain pending. Final review is an author self-review, not independent review.

Final export-verifier review: negative tests showed that changing private manifest task ID, schema, admission flag, public-file list or limits was not rejected. The verifier now reconstructs and compares the entire canonical manifest byte-for-byte, in addition to public payloads, file sets and source binding. All five tamper regressions pass.
